+
+## Blackfin / bcore comparison
+
+Select **ARM / Blackfin / direct Wasm** to run the same five kernels through
+`bfin-elf-gcc` and [op1emu/bcore](https://github.com/op1emu/bcore)'s Wasm backend.
+The original ARM / x86 comparison remains available. Each group has three paths
+so every runtime occupies each of the three order positions once per case.
+The Blackfin mode keeps the same Quick/Extended counts, seeds, independent
+reference checksums, six-call protocol, fresh Workers and paired ratios.
+`bcore / ARM > 1` means the ARM path takes less time.
+
+Blackfin uses GCC 4.5.3 (ADI 2014R1_45-RC2), `-O2 -mcpu=bf532`, scalar settings
+and no libc. This is **not a same-compiler comparison**: the other two paths use
+Clang. GCC 4.5 does not support Clang's vectorization switches or
+`-ffp-contract=off`; vectorization is disabled with `-fno-tree-vectorize`, and
+Blackfin's binary64 operations call libgcc helpers (including `__muldf3` and
+`__adddf3`), with no hardware FMA or host floating-point shortcut. The float
+result therefore includes software floating-point costs, not just translation.
+Read integer/memory and floating-point results separately.
+
+The pinned bcore revision is `df7aee68589424364e74d6cee4def5269721e3e6`.
+The host follows OP-1's web defaults: copy-and-patch with hints and split
+stencils; 10 cold starts, batches of 32 blocks or 65,536 packets; chaining,
+lazy state and loop blocks. After startup the webpage Worker enables remote
+LLVM promotion at threshold `100000`, size reference `16`. The compiler Worker
+warms each generated function before sending the module back. Tier 1 uses IR
+O1 / codegen O2; the light tier-1 pipeline is off. LLVM is split into a deferred
+module, loaded by the compiler Worker, and only by the execution Worker if an
+unsupported packet requires it. Profiling, chained-block diagnostic counts and
+verification are disabled. Runs yield through MessageChannel after an 8 ms
+slice, checking the clock every 32 dispatches or 100,000 guest packets. The
+chain cap is `2^24`; host cycles, IDLE waits and `SYSCFG=0x30` match OP-1.
+RAM uses a page table: bcore cannot use copy-and-patch with FlatMemory's raw
+inline path. This measures bare-metal C, not the full OP-1 firmware/device
+simulation. These settings are taken from `web/src/op1_web.cpp`, `web/worker.js`,
+`web/compile-worker.js` and `src/cpu/cpu.cpp` at op1emu revision
+`9ba8d234b561efc77f1894f142b39efbd1f7a2db`. Its host builds with the
+upstream-supported Emscripten 4.0.9 and Wasm LLVM 23.1.2, versus the ARM host's
+Emscripten 6.0.13. Host code uses Release O3 and the upstream-recommended O1
+link. The manifest records these differences.
+
+Blackfin has the same 16 MiB guest RAM, entry `0x10000`, stack `0xff0000` and
+mailbox `0xf000`. Guest writes of phase 0/1 to `0x01000000` record real host
+monotonic timestamps using `emscripten_get_now`; writing the exit status to
+`0x01000004` stops execution. Initialization and input resets remain outside
+the timed interval. The host starts with 128 MiB Wasm memory and may grow;
+LLVM also compiles guest blocks to separate Wasm modules. This is not a claim
+of equal total host memory consumption. Loading, first-call compilation and
+warm measurements are shown separately. The execution and compiler Workers
+have separate 128 MiB initial memories; the report records both. The split
+host downloads roughly 43 MiB of main Wasm and 29 MiB of deferred LLVM,
+uncompressed. It loads only when selected. OP-1's Brotli/Service Worker download
+cache is not replicated, so preparation timings do not compare download UX.
+
+Two fixed warmup calls may finish before a background promotion arrives.
+Every observation exports submitted/installed counts, the first installation's
+marker index (0–12; use the installed count to distinguish no installation)
+and installations before measured calls.
+The page shows the counts in its details. Timings include time spent yielding
+and any tier changes within a call; they do not claim pure tier-1 steady state.
+A failed compiler Worker invalidates this benchmark, rather than silently
+reporting a slower tier-0-only path. See the [configuration audit](OP1-CONFIG.md).
+
+The Pages workflow includes this path. To add it to a local `browser_page`
+distribution on Linux x86_64 (Node 24+, Python 3.10+, CMake):
+
+```sh
+bash examples/c-bench/setup-bcore.sh build-bcore-tools
+python3 examples/c-bench/build-bcore.py \
+  --bcore-source build-bcore-tools/bcore \
+  --bfin-gcc build-bcore-tools/bfin-elf/bin/bfin-elf-gcc \
+  --emsdk build-bcore-tools/wasm-tools/emsdk-4.0.9 \
+  --llvm build-bcore-tools/wasm-tools/llvm-23.1.2 \
+  --dist build-em/examples/browser/dist --cache build-c-bench-bcore
+```
+
+Alternatively pass existing toolchain paths. The builder requires the pinned,
+tracked-clean bcore checkout and builds a source snapshot in its own cache;
+it does not change the checkout or reuse unknown upstream build artifacts.
+It retains guest ELF/disassembly and packages bcore's source, MIT license,
+LLVM/Emscripten/runtime notices, GCC GPLv3 and GCC Runtime Library Exception.
+`c-bench-bcore-manifest.json` binds the compiler, libgcc, inputs and artifacts.
+Both the page and Worker reject mismatched C sources, and the Worker verifies
+the bytes of every bcore executable before running them. Source packaging also
+rejects stale Blackfin inputs/artifacts. Rebuild both guest sets after changing
+shared sources. A local build without the optional artifacts reports a missing
+resource if Blackfin is selected.
+
+Run correctness checks and collect paired performance evidence:
+
+```sh
+node tests/browser/verify_bcore_bench.mjs /path/to/chrome \
+  build-em/examples/browser/dist build-c-bench-bcore/checks.json
+node examples/c-bench/measure.mjs --browser /path/to/chrome \
+  --dist build-em/examples/browser/dist --comparison bcore --profile quick \
+  --sessions 5 --output NEW_RESULTS
+```
+
+Quick is useful for this comparison because binary64 software arithmetic is
+much slower than the integer cases; Extended remains available with unchanged
+work counts. Use Chrome/Edge 133+ with Wasm tail calls (tested with Chrome 149). The verifier executes all Quick page rounds,
+all six Blackfin calls for every Extended case, corrupt-asset rejection,
+cancellation, visibility invalidation and mobile layout.
+
+The earlier [LLVM O2 five-session comparison](../../internals/measurements/2026-10-31-bcore-c-bench-performance/README.md)
+is retained as a separate, non-default configuration. It must not be presented
+as the default copy-and-patch result.
+
+The [web-default five-session comparison](../../internals/measurements/2026-10-31-bcore-web-defaults-performance/README.md)
+uses copy-and-patch with background LLVM promotion and records the actual
+compilation stage of each observation. It supersedes the LLVM-only setup as
+the page default.
+
+## Native backend comparison
+
+The [native harness](native/README.md) runs the same ARM/Blackfin guest binaries
+through their x86-64 native JITs, without Wasm. It retains the fixed-work,
+six-call checksum protocol and paired repetitions, with separate bcore native
+default, CP-without-promotion and LLVM-only configurations.
+The [five-session native report](../../internals/measurements/2026-11-01-native-c-bench-performance/README.md)
+covers the four integer/memory cases at Extended counts, including raw samples,
+first-call costs and exact build provenance.
